#!/bin/sh
#
# MiSTer FIRST-BOOT INSTALLER /init  (TASKS.md P5.3; PLAN §8; ADR 0017 §4; ADR 0020)
#
# THIS FILE CAN BRICK THE BOARD. Read this whole header, ADR 0020, and
# docs/boot-chain.md §2.1/§3.1/§5 before touching a single line.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this is
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shipped sdcard.img is a *small* dd/Etcher-writable card. On the very first
# boot the stock U-Boot (uboot.img, byte-identical, in the 0xA2 boot partition)
# loads /linux/zImage_dtb off the FAT32 data partition (p1). On the pristine card
# that zImage_dtb is the INSTALLER kernel: our 6.18 kernel relinked with THIS
# initramfs embedded. So this /init runs, as PID 1, entirely from RAM (the
# initramfs is a ramfs; nothing we do to the SD card pulls the rug out from under
# the running system -- that is the whole reason the reformat-in-place trick works).
#
# MiSTer's data partition is exFAT, which Linux cannot grow in place (no resize
# tool exists for exFAT). So "auto-expand to fill the card" is NOT a filesystem
# grow -- it is a REFORMAT, exactly like mr-fusion (MiSTer-devel/mr-fusion,
# builder/scripts/S99install-MiSTer.sh, which this file is adapted from). We:
#
#   1. mount the shipped FAT32 data partition (p1) read-only and work out which
#      state the card is in (see RECOVERABILITY),
#   2. read the payload: check it is complete, and capture any user pre-seed the
#      user dropped at the FAT root before first boot,
#   3. copy /mister-payload/ into a RAM tmpfs, ALONGSIDE the installer's own
#      /linux/zImage_dtb and the FAT root's /menu.rbf -- the two files that let a
#      half-finished card boot back into this installer,
#   4. sfdisk-repartition the WHOLE card to full size: p1 = exFAT data filling the
#      card minus a small reserved tail, p2 = type 0xA2 for U-Boot,
#   5. dd uboot.img raw to the new 0xA2 p2 IMMEDIATELY -- seconds after the
#      repartition the card can reach U-Boot again,
#   6. mkfs.exfat -n MiSTer_Data on the new p1 (in-kernel exfat driver, ADR 0019 --
#      we mount it with `mount -t exfat`, NO fuse, unlike mr-fusion),
#   7. write the INSTALLER's own zImage_dtb and menu.rbf onto that fresh p1 FIRST,
#      before any payload byte -- from this instant a power cut boots straight back
#      into this installer rather than into nothing,
#   8. copy the payload back out of RAM into mister-payload.part/ and, once it is
#      complete, RENAME that directory to mister-payload/ (a directory-entry
#      update, which is what makes a partial copy detectable on the next boot),
#   9. `zcat` mister-payload/linux/linux.img.gz -> linux/linux.img directly onto
#      the card (the 512 MiB image lands on DISK, never in RAM -- see the MEMORY
#      note below), with the payload left complete for the whole decompression,
#  10. COMMIT: drop the now-redundant .gz, rename the payload's entries into their
#      final places at the root, apply the pre-seed, write a UNIQUE per-board MAC
#      into <data>/linux/u-boot.txt, rename the REAL kernel over linux/zImage_dtb
#      as the very last act, then sync + reboot.
#
# Those ten are exactly the steps the console splash counts off (1/10 .. 10/10),
# so they are what a user with a serial cable sees. There is ONE numbering in this
# file; keep every reference below on it.
#
# The reformat writes the REAL /linux/zImage_dtb (from the payload) onto p1, so the
# next boot loads the real MiSTer kernel, not this installer. That kernel-swap is
# the primary re-run guard; the payload-dir/linux.img check below is belt.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECOVERABILITY -- why steps 5 and 7 sit where they do (issue #185)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Until 2026-09 the order was: repartition, format, copy back, expand, pre-seed,
# MAC, dd uboot.img. From mkfs.exfat onwards -- which is very nearly the whole of
# a ~1-minute install on a real card -- a power cut left a card that could not
# boot at all: sfdisk had moved the 0xA2 partition to the tail of the medium with
# no SPL in it, and the format had just destroyed the only kernel on p1, with the
# replacement not written until the very last step. The installer is also visually
# silent for that whole minute (see WHY THERE IS NO HDMI SPLASH below), so the
# window in which a user is most likely to pull the power was exactly the window
# that destroyed the card. Issue #185 reported precisely that.
#
# The order above attacks the harm rather than the visibility. Two writes are
# hoisted to the front so the card becomes self-recovering almost immediately:
#
#   * uboot.img goes into the new p2 the moment that partition exists (step 5), so
#     the BootROM can find an SPL again seconds after the table changed;
#   * the installer's OWN kernel and menu.rbf go onto the fresh p1 before any
#     payload byte does (step 7), so U-Boot's `load mmc 0:1 ... zImage_dtb` finds
#     something to boot -- this installer, which simply runs again.
#
# Everything after that is arranged so that at any moment a power cut can happen
# the card is in exactly one of four self-describing states:
#
#   mister-payload/ present and complete
#       -> a full, unattended re-install on the next boot. This is the normal
#          interrupted state and the whole point of the reorder; it costs the user
#          one more run and nothing else.
#   mister-payload.part/ present instead
#       -> the copy-back was cut short. The bytes it was copying from lived in RAM
#          and are gone, so there is nothing left on the card to install from:
#          halt with an honest message, never a reboot loop.
#   neither present, and no linux/linux.img either
#       -> formatted but never populated: the cut landed between mkfs.exfat and
#          the copy-back. Same verdict as above and for the same reason -- the
#          source lived in RAM. Halt and say to re-flash.
#   neither present, linux/linux.img there
#       -> installed. Stop and say so (the "already provisioned" guard).
#
# The COMMIT POINT is the single `mv` of the real linux/zImage_dtb over the
# installer's own, at the very end. Before it the card boots this installer; after
# it the card boots MiSTer.
#
# TWO windows are left where a power cut still costs a re-flash, and they are both
# short because of where the writes above were moved to:
#
#   * mkfs.exfat until the installer kernel lands on the new p1 -- a few seconds on
#     a real card. This is the only HARD one: for that moment the card has a
#     bootloader but no kernel on p1, so it cannot get to Linux at all. Note what
#     is NOT in it: sfdisk only rewrites the MBR, and the new p1 still starts at
#     LBA 2048, so between sfdisk and mkfs.exfat the shipped FAT32 filesystem --
#     installer kernel, payload and all -- is still there and still readable under
#     the enlarged partition entry. An interrupt in THAT window simply re-runs
#     (scripts/test-installer-recovery.sh proves it).
#   * the commit phase, from the `rm` of linux/linux.img.gz (which is what first
#     makes mister-payload/ incomplete -- it precedes every rename) to the final
#     `mv`. The installer still boots and still says what happened; it just has no
#     source left to finish from. Directory-entry updates on an already-written
#     filesystem, so fractions of a second.
#
# Neither is zero; together they are the smallest this can be made without writing
# every byte twice, against an old ordering whose window was the entire ~1-minute
# install. Do not put anything slow inside either one.
#
# HDMI visibility is addressed separately -- THE HDMI SPLASH in the splash
# section below (2026-09-21, ADR 0020 section 9): this ordering shrinks the
# consequence of pulling the power; the picture shrinks the temptation to.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The load-bearing facts (get one wrong and you brick a card)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * PARTITION SLOTS ARE FIXED. Both the shipped card and the final card use
#   p1 = data, p2 = 0xA2 boot -- because the stock U-Boot's compiled-in env has
#   `mmcroot=/dev/mmcblk0p1` and `load mmc 0:1 ... $bootimage`, i.e. it ALWAYS reads
#   the kernel from partition 1 (docs/boot-chain.md §3.1 entries 16-17, §4). BootROM
#   independently scans the MBR for the first 0xA2 partition (§2.1), which is p2.
#   The final sfdisk table below MUST reproduce this or the reformatted card is dead.
# * THE 0xA2 PARTITION MOVES. Repartitioning creates a NEW p2 with no SPL in it.
#   Until `dd uboot.img -> p2` succeeds the card CANNOT boot. That dd is the single
#   most brick-critical step; it is verified and, if it fails, we scream and hand the
#   user a recovery hint (re-flash sdcard.img) rather than rebooting into a brick.
#   It is also the FIRST thing done after sfdisk, for the reason in RECOVERABILITY
#   above -- do not let a later edit push it back down the file.
# * uboot.txt MUST END IN A NEWLINE. U-Boot's `env import -t` (no size arg) scans for
#   '\n' followed by '\0' (docs/boot-chain.md §4, u-boot cmd/nvedit.c:1049-1066). We
#   printf a trailing newline.
# * MEMORY IS CAPPED AT 511 MiB. We boot on the STOCK uboot.img, whose compiled-in
#   `mmcboot` hardcodes `mem=511M memmap=513M$511M` as a LITERAL string and never
#   interpolates a `mem=` env var (docs/boot-chain.md §4). So this installer sees
#   ~511 MiB, NOT the full 1 GiB of physical DDR, and NO shipped `linux/u-boot.txt`
#   can lift that cap -- only rebuilding U-Boot could, which is out of scope. The
#   payload transits RAM (step 3), so it MUST fit under ~511 MiB. The single item
#   that would blow that budget is linux.img: a 512 MiB apparent-size ext4 image,
#   stored NON-sparse on FAT32, i.e. bigger than the whole address space. So it does
#   NOT transit RAM as a 512 MiB blob -- scripts/mk-sdcard.sh ships it GZIPPED
#   (linux/linux.img.gz, ~80 MiB of real data); we copy that small .gz through RAM
#   like everything else and, only AFTER copy-back, `zcat` it straight onto the
#   exFAT card (step 9 below), so the full 512 MiB is written to DISK, never held
#   in RAM. The rest of the base (~60 MB: MiSTer, menu.rbf, soundfonts, scripts, the
#   real kernel) fits with wide margin; the optional _Console cores (~150-200
#   MB) may NOT, so we measure free RAM first and, if the full payload will not fit,
#   copy the base and SKIP the cores with a loud message rather than OOM-bricking.
#   update_all.sh on the installed card fetches cores anyway.
#   Two files that are NOT part of mister-payload/ ride in the same tmpfs: the FAT
#   root's own linux/zImage_dtb (THIS installer's kernel, ~11 MB) and menu.rbf
#   (~2.4 MB), staged so step 7 can put the card back on its feet. Both are
#   measured into the budget below; together they are ~13 MB against ~400 MB of
#   headroom, and they are never skipped -- the cores are what gives way first.
#   One wrinkle in that measurement, harmless but worth knowing: `du` reports
#   ALLOCATED blocks, and a RE-RUN reads the payload off the full-size exFAT this
#   installer made (128 KiB clusters on a 64 GB card) rather than the shipped
#   FAT32 (32 KiB), so the same tree measures bigger on the second pass. The error
#   is in the safe direction -- it can only make a re-run skip cores a first run
#   kept, never the reverse -- and update_all.sh fetches them anyway.
# * DEFENSIVE LIKE THE STAGE-1 init. Same contract as
#   board/mister/common/initramfs-overlay/init: early proc/sys/dev mounts, parse
#   the cmdline (never hardcode the device), and on ANY fatal error print a banner
#   and drop to a RESPAWNING rescue shell. PID 1 must NEVER exit (that is an instant
#   kernel panic). A half-reformatted card must give a diagnosable message, not a
#   black brick.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# External programs this init needs (beyond the stage-1 busybox applet set)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the installer Buildroot config (configs/mister_installer_defconfig):
#   sfdisk                    (BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX + _BINARIES)
#   mkfs.exfat                (BR2_PACKAGE_EXFATPROGS)
#   itsalive                  (BR2_PACKAGE_ITSALIVE -- the HDMI splash. OPTIONAL at
#                              run time: probed for, every call under `timeout`,
#                              every status only logged; see the splash section)
# Provided by busybox in EITHER stage-1's set or installer-busybox.config:
#   sh(ash+test+math) mount umount mkdir printf sleep sync   (already in stage-1)
#   findfs setsid cttyhack cat ls tail dmesg                 (already in stage-1)
#   cp du dd cut reboot blkid blockdev hexdump rm mv timeout (installer must add)
# The applet budget is real but it is NOT fixed: `rm` was added in PR #76 because
# /init genuinely needed it (step 10) and it costs ~1 KB, and `mv` was added for
# the recoverable ordering above (the .part rename and the commit renames are the
# whole reason a partial copy is detectable, and a rename is also what keeps the
# commit from re-writing 150+ MiB). If a future revision
# needs an applet, turn its CONFIG_ symbol on in installer-busybox.config and
# document why in that file's header -- do not contort the shell around its
# absence. Do check the symbol actually exists in the BusyBox being built:
# kconfig silently discards unknown symbols, so a typo reads as "not set".
# Note the budget applies to APPLETS only -- `true` and `false` are unconditional
# ash BUILTINS (shell/ash.c builtintab, outside any #if), so `|| true` is safe
# here even though CONFIG_TRUE is off; that symbol governs only /bin/true.
# (blkid/blockdev/hexdump may come from busybox or util-linux; only sfdisk+mkfs.exfat
# must be real external binaries.) NOTE: basename/dirname/od are deliberately NOT
# built -- /init uses POSIX ${var%/*}/${var##*/} parameter expansion in their place,
# and hexdump instead of od for the MAC. Everything here is POSIX sh / busybox ash;
# no bashisms.
# The console/LED half of the first-boot splash (see its own section below)
# deliberately adds NOTHING to this list: it is built entirely from shell
# builtins plus printf and sleep, both of which are already here. The HDMI half
# (2026-09-21) adds exactly two things: the `itsalive` binary, and BusyBox
# `timeout`, which brackets every call to it so a wedged tool can never hold the
# install. Read that section's "APPLET BUDGET" note before editing it -- several
# obvious implementations need applets we do not ship.
#
set -u

# --- Tunables ---------------------------------------------------------------
PAYLOAD_DIR="mister-payload"           # subdir on the shipped p1 holding the real card
PAYLOAD_PART_DIR="mister-payload.part" # the SAME tree while it is still being written.
                                       # The copy-back lands here and is renamed to
                                       # PAYLOAD_DIR only once complete, so "an interrupted
                                       # copy" and "a payload worth re-installing from" are
                                       # two different directory names rather than a guess
                                       # (see RECOVERABILITY in the header).
CORES_DIR="_Console"                   # payload top-level dir skipped first under RAM pressure
DATA_LABEL="MiSTer_Data"               # exFAT volume label the final card must carry
RESERVED_SECTORS=8192                  # 512-byte sectors given to the 0xA2 boot partition
                                       # (8192*512 = 4 MiB; uboot.img is ~503 KiB, so ample).
                                       # This is the EXACT p2 size, matching genimage-sdcard.cfg's
                                       # `size = 4M` on the shipped image -- see the sfdisk step.
P1_START_SECTORS=2048                  # p1's start LBA = 1 MiB alignment, matching sfdisk's own
                                       # default AND genimage-sdcard.cfg's `align = 1M`. Named
                                       # explicitly so the sfdisk layout is deterministic and p2
                                       # ends up EXACTLY RESERVED_SECTORS (not RESERVED-2048).
RAM_RESERVE_KB=98304                   # 96 MiB of RAM kept free for the kernel + our own
                                       # working set; the payload tmpfs may use the rest.
DEV_WAIT=30                            # seconds to wait for a block device to enumerate

# --- where the RAM copy lives ----------------------------------------------
# One tmpfs with two subdirectories, because two unrelated things are staged in
# it. RAM_PAYLOAD is mister-payload/ verbatim and its contents are iterated with a
# glob; RAM_INSTALLER holds the two recovery files, which must NOT appear in that
# glob. Separate directories, not a naming convention, so the glob cannot pick
# them up by accident.
RAM_DIR="/tmp/stage"
RAM_PAYLOAD="$RAM_DIR/payload"
RAM_INSTALLER="$RAM_DIR/installer"

# --- rescue-banner state (all pre-initialised so `set -u` is safe in rescue) --
cmdline=""; root_arg=""; DATA_PART=""; DISK=""; P1=""; P2=""
fat_type=""; stage="starting up"; cores_skipped="no"

# TEST-ONLY interruption knob; see stop_after() far below. Inert unless the kernel
# command line carries mister_installer_stop_after=, which the stock U-Boot's
# compiled-in bootargs never does.
stop_after_stage=""

# ===========================================================================
#  FIRST-BOOT SPLASH -- HDMI picture + console progress UI + hps_led0 heartbeat
# ===========================================================================
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# On a pristine card everything below takes tens of seconds during which the
# board looks completely dead: no menu, and -- without a serial adapter -- no
# output whatsoever. A user reasonably concludes it has hung and pulls the
# power, and the window they pull it in is very likely to be the reformat,
# which is exactly the window that leaves an unbootable card. Every line in
# this section exists to make "it is working, wait" observable.
#
# THE HDMI SPLASH (2026-09-21, ADR 0020 section 9, issue #185)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Until 2026-09-21 this header said "WHY THERE IS NO HDMI SPLASH" and listed
# three blockers; the reasoning is kept because two of the three are what the
# picture still depends on. mr-fusion shows a PNG with `fbv` because it ships
# its OWN U-Boot, a Terasic DTB and a Terasic framebuffer BITSTREAM whose frame
# reader scans DDR out to HDMI by itself. We boot the STOCK uboot.img
# byte-identical (ADR 0017) with our kernel, and on that stack:
#
#   1. The fabric needs a bitstream or the ADV7513 never gets a pixel clock.
#      SOLVED by ADR 0020 section 7: menu.rbf ships at the FAT root, so U-Boot's
#      `fpgaload` puts it in the fabric before Linux starts. Step 7 below keeps
#      it there on a re-run. This is the precondition; on its own it puts the
#      menu core's static on screen and nothing else.
#   2. /dev/fb0 (MiSTer_fb, docs/abi-contract.md section 4) is a window on DDR
#      that the FPGA frame reader only scans out once something programs it,
#      which used to mean Main_MiSTer (fb_cmd0/fb_cmd1 via /dev/MiSTer_cmd, a
#      FIFO Main itself creates) -- and Main cannot run in this initramfs.
#      SOLVED by `itsalive` (package/itsalive, github.com/mcfbytes/ItsAlive_MiSTer):
#      a static tool that runs Main's own register sequences -- video PLL and
#      timings over the fabric mailbox, the ADV7513 over i2c, then UIO_SET_FBUF
#      to point the frame reader at /dev/fb0 -- transcribed from Main_MiSTer and
#      needing only the menu.rbf from (1). One call, `itsalive up`, and the
#      framebuffer is live. Verified on a DE10-Nano on 2026-09-21 with no Main
#      on the card (ItsAlive_MiSTer docs/testlogs/2026-09-21-rig-first-light.md).
#   3. The kernel console is `console=ttyS0,115200` only, so the CONSOLE text
#      never reaches the screen. IRRELEVANT for the picture: the artwork is
#      blitted straight into /dev/fb0 (`itsalive image`), and the few lines the
#      terminal states show are written to /dev/tty1 (`itsalive say`), which
#      fbcon paints whatever console= says.
#
# What is drawn: a full-screen 1280x720 image -- MiSTer Kun, "INSTALLING...",
# "DO NOT POWER OFF", "it reboots itself when done" -- rendered per mode as
# gzipped raw BGRX8888 into /usr/share/mister-installer/ at build time by
# installer-post-build.sh from the PNGs in board/mister/de10nano/
# installer-splash/ (sources and licence there). It is the first thing /init
# does once /proc, /sys and /dev are mounted -- ahead of the serial banner and
# the LED -- so it is on screen as soon as the kernel hands over, long before
# the point of no return, and stays until the reboot. The three terminal states
# (failed / already installed / done) replace it with a few lines of text.
# Progress is NOT mirrored to the screen: the console UI and a blit fight over
# the same pixels, and "do not power off, wait a minute" is the whole message.
#
# HDMI IS AS OPTIONAL AS THE LED. The binary may be absent (a config without
# BR2_PACKAGE_ITSALIVE), the fabric may hold no bitstream (QEMU; a card whose
# menu.rbf went missing -- exit 10), the sink may want a mode we do not send
# (`mister_installer_video=480p` on the cmdline picks the other one), or the
# tool may fail in a way nobody predicted. Every call is probed first, runs
# under `timeout`, and has its status LOGGED and nothing else. A missing
# picture costs the user a minute of doubt; a splash that could hang PID 1
# would cost them the card. Read "A SPLASH MUST NEVER BE ABLE TO FAIL AN
# INSTALL" below; it applies to every line of the HDMI half.
#
# WHAT WE DO
# ----------
#  1. A progress UI on the serial console -- the documented triage channel:
#     banner, numbered steps, a bar, percentage, elapsed time and a spinner.
#  2. The DE10-Nano's HPS LED at /sys/class/leds/hps_led0 (DTS `gpio-leds hps0`,
#     &portb 24, linux-patches/0004-dts-de10nano-MiSTer.patch). This is HPS-side
#     GPIO driven by the kernel's gpio-leds driver, so unlike the eight LED0-7
#     user LEDs -- which hang off the FPGA fabric and which nothing in this
#     initramfs drives -- it needs no bitstream. It is the only install-time
#     signal a user with no serial cable AND no picture can see (a dark HDMI,
#     or any installer before 2026-09-21), so it carries the state:
#
#         blinking ~0.5 Hz .. installing; all is well, leave it alone
#         solid on ......... STOPPED and wants attention: either the install
#                            failed, or the card was already installed. Either
#                            way the console says which. This is the ONLY state
#                            that means trouble, which is why nothing on the
#                            healthy path is allowed to park the LED lit.
#         off .............. between two long phases, or finished and rebooting.
#                            Only the long steps carry a heartbeat, so brief dark
#                            gaps mid-install are normal; a dark LED never means
#                            trouble, and the reboot into MiSTer is what tells
#                            you the "finished" case apart.
#
#     We take the LED over (trigger=none), which costs the DTS default `mmc0`
#     activity flicker for the duration. That is the entire point: erratic
#     activity flicker is indistinguishable from an ordinary boot, a metronome is
#     not. Nothing here persists -- `linux,default-trigger = "mmc0"` is a DTS
#     property re-applied at probe, so the installed card gets its normal SD
#     activity light back on the very next boot.
#  3. The HDMI picture described above, via itsalive -- see the "HDMI splash"
#     functions at the end of this section.
#
# A SPLASH MUST NEVER BE ABLE TO FAIL AN INSTALL. There is no `set -e` here; all
# LED writes are guarded and the sysfs paths are probed, never assumed; every
# itsalive call is bracketed by `timeout` and its exit status is only logged.
# If none of this can draw, the install still runs to completion exactly as
# before.
#
# APPLET BUDGET -- read before editing. The installer busybox
# (installer-busybox.config) ships NO rm, touch, kill, usleep, date, seq or tr,
# and its `sleep` is integer-only: CONFIG_FEATURE_FANCY_SLEEP is off, so a
# `sleep 0.2` would parse as 0 and BUSY-SPIN a core for the whole install.
# Consequently:
#   * the heartbeat child is stopped by TRUNCATING a flag file with `: >` (a pure
#     shell redirection) and reaped with the ash builtin `wait` -- not kill (no
#     applet, and ash's builtin kill is gated behind CONFIG_KILL) and not rm.
#   * elapsed time is read from /proc/uptime with the `read` builtin -- not date.
#   * the bar is built by string concatenation in a loop -- not seq/tr/printf.
#   * every frame delay is a whole number of seconds.
#   * ASCII art is emitted as `printf '%s\n' 'line' 'line' ...` (printf reuses the
#     format for each argument), so no line is ever escape-processed. Do not
#     switch it to a heredoc or to escapes in a format string.
#   * the HDMI half is the one deliberate exception to "no applets": it needs
#     `timeout` (CONFIG_TIMEOUT, on since 2026-09-21 -- see installer-busybox.
#     config's header for the costing) and `zcat`, which the linux.img expansion
#     already ships. Every itsalive invocation goes through splash_hdmi_run,
#     which is where the timeout lives; do not call the binary directly.
# >>> SPLASH SECTION BEGIN -- scripts/test-installer-splash.sh extracts everything
# between these two markers VERBATIM and sources it under a POSIX shell against a
# stubbed /proc/uptime and /sys/class/leds. Keep the section self-contained: it
# must not reference anything defined outside it, or that test stops working.
SPLASH_TOTAL=10               # user-visible steps; keep in sync with splash_step calls
SPLASH_BAR_CELLS=28           # bar width -- the whole status line must fit 80 cols
SPLASH_FLAG=/run/splash.run   # non-empty => the heartbeat child should keep running

# All pre-initialised so `set -u` is safe even if rescue() fires during the early
# mounts, i.e. before splash_init has run. splash_tty=0 until proven otherwise
# keeps every drawing routine a no-op until then.
splash_n=0; splash_label="starting up"; splash_bar=""; splash_pct=0
splash_frame=0; splash_t0=0; splash_tty=0
splash_led=""; splash_led_on=0; splash_pulsing=0

# Whole seconds since boot. /proc/uptime is "1234.56 5678.90"; `read` splits it.
splash_uptime() {
	_up=0
	read -r _up _rest < /proc/uptime 2>/dev/null || _up=0
	_up="${_up%%.*}"
	case "$_up" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _up=0 ;; esac
	printf '%s' "$_up"
}

# Elapsed since splash_init, as "0m42s".
splash_elapsed() {
	_e=$(( $(splash_uptime) - splash_t0 ))
	[ "$_e" -ge 0 ] || _e=0
	printf '%dm%02ds' "$((_e / 60))" "$((_e % 60))"
}

# Find the HPS LED and take it off its DTS default trigger so brightness writes
# stick. Restricted to hps_led* on purpose: other /sys/class/leds entries on a
# MiSTer belong to USB gamepads (patches 0032/0033/0042, plus hid-nintendo's
# mainline-named player LEDs) and blinking a
# user's controller to report SD-card progress would be nonsense.
splash_led_init() {
	splash_led=""
	for _c in /sys/class/leds/hps_led0 /sys/class/leds/hps_led*; do
		[ -w "$_c/brightness" ] || continue
		splash_led="$_c"
		break
	done
	[ -n "$splash_led" ] || return 0
	printf 'none' > "$splash_led/trigger" 2>/dev/null || true
	splash_led_set 0
}

splash_led_set() {
	[ -n "$splash_led" ] || return 0
	printf '%s' "$1" > "$splash_led/brightness" 2>/dev/null || true
	splash_led_on="$1"
}

splash_led_toggle() {
	if [ "$splash_led_on" = 1 ]; then splash_led_set 0; else splash_led_set 1; fi
}

# Pre-render the bar for the current step; the per-second frames only redraw the
# spinner and the clock, so this runs once per step, not once per frame.
splash_render_bar() {
	_i=0; splash_bar=""
	while [ "$_i" -lt "$SPLASH_BAR_CELLS" ]; do
		if [ "$_i" -lt "$1" ]; then splash_bar="$splash_bar#"; else splash_bar="$splash_bar."; fi
		_i=$((_i + 1))
	done
}

# Always `printf '%s' <char>`, never a bare format string: '-' would otherwise be
# eaten as an option and the backslash frame would be escape-processed. The
# backslash is written "\\" (double quotes) rather than '\' -- both are a single
# literal backslash in POSIX sh, but the quoted form does not trip SC1003.
splash_spin_char() {
	case $((splash_frame % 4)) in
	0) printf '%s' '|'  ;;
	1) printf '%s' '/'  ;;
	2) printf '%s' '-'  ;;
	*) printf '%s' "\\" ;;
	esac
}

# The live, in-place status line. No trailing newline: it is rewritten with \r
# every frame and erased to end-of-line so a shorter label cannot leave debris.
splash_line() {
	[ "$splash_tty" = 1 ] || return 0
	printf '\r  [%s] %3d%%  %d/%d  %s  %s %s\033[K' \
		"$splash_bar" "$splash_pct" "$splash_n" "$SPLASH_TOTAL" \
		"$(splash_elapsed)" "$(splash_spin_char)" "$splash_label"
}

splash_clear_line() {
	[ "$splash_tty" = 1 ] || return 0
	printf '\r\033[K'
}

splash_banner() {
	printf '%s\n' \
'' \
'================================================================' \
'        .------.' \
'       /  ____ |        M i S T e r' \
'      |  |____||        F I R S T - B O O T   S E T U P' \
'      |        |' \
'      |________|        preparing your SD card' \
'================================================================' \
'  This usually takes well under a minute.' \
'' \
'  *** Do NOT power off or remove the card while the board LED' \
'      is blinking. ***' \
'================================================================' \
''
}

splash_init() {
	splash_t0="$(splash_uptime)"
	if [ -t 1 ]; then splash_tty=1; else splash_tty=0; fi
	splash_led_init
	splash_banner
	splash_step 0 "starting up"
}

# splash_step N "label" -- advance the user-visible progress. Always stops the
# heartbeat first so the child can never interleave with what we are about to
# print. On a non-tty (a captured log) we emit one plain line per step instead of
# animating, so log files stay readable.
splash_step() {
	splash_pulse_stop
	splash_n="$1"
	splash_label="$2"
	splash_pct=$(( splash_n * 100 / SPLASH_TOTAL ))
	splash_render_bar "$(( splash_n * SPLASH_BAR_CELLS / SPLASH_TOTAL ))"
	if [ "$splash_tty" = 1 ]; then
		splash_line
	else
		printf '[installer] step %d/%d: %s\n' "$splash_n" "$SPLASH_TOTAL" "$splash_label"
	fi
}

# One animation frame. Called two ways: inline from the polling loops that
# already sleep 1 (so they animate for free, with no child process), and from the
# heartbeat child that brackets the long blocking commands.
splash_tick() {
	splash_frame=$((splash_frame + 1))
	splash_led_toggle
	splash_line
}

splash_heartbeat() {
	while [ -s "$SPLASH_FLAG" ]; do
		splash_tick
		sleep 1
	done
}

splash_pulse_start() {
	[ "$splash_pulsing" = 0 ] || return 0
	printf 'run' > "$SPLASH_FLAG" 2>/dev/null || return 0
	splash_pulsing=1
	splash_heartbeat &
}

# Truncating the flag ends the child's loop within one frame. We only `wait` if
# the truncation is OBSERVED to have worked: if it somehow did not, the child
# would never exit and a bare `wait` would hang the installer forever -- a
# cosmetic bug must not be able to do that. /run is ramfs, so the fallback path
# is close to unreachable; it costs one `[` to be certain.
splash_pulse_stop() {
	[ "$splash_pulsing" = 1 ] || return 0
	splash_pulsing=0
	if : > "$SPLASH_FLAG" 2>/dev/null && [ ! -s "$SPLASH_FLAG" ]; then
		wait
	fi
	# The child toggled the LED in a subshell, so our copy of splash_led_on is
	# stale. Re-assert a known state or the next toggle can be a no-op frame.
	#
	# That state must be OFF, not on. The short steps between two long ones are
	# not bracketed by a heartbeat, so whatever we park here is what the LED shows
	# across them -- and a SUSTAINED solid-on is the one signal reserved for "the
	# board has stopped and wants a human" (splash_fail / splash_halt_ok). Parking
	# on would light that signal in the middle of a perfectly healthy install.
	splash_led_set 0
}

splash_done() {
	splash_pulse_stop
	splash_n="$SPLASH_TOTAL"; splash_pct=100; splash_label="done"
	splash_render_bar "$SPLASH_BAR_CELLS"
	splash_clear_line
	printf '%s\n' \
'' \
'================================================================' \
'  INSTALL COMPLETE' \
'================================================================'
	printf '  [%s] 100%%   finished in %s\n' "$splash_bar" "$(splash_elapsed)"
	printf '%s\n' \
'' \
'  Rebooting into MiSTer now. The LED goes dark until the menu' \
'  core comes up.' \
'================================================================' \
''
	splash_led_set 0
	splash_hdmi_say --clear 'MiSTer first-time setup: DONE' '' 'Rebooting into MiSTer now.'
}

# Both terminal states leave the LED SOLID ON: the board has stopped and wants a
# human. Which of the two it is, is what the console banner is for -- and, since
# the HDMI splash, what the screen says too: the "installing" picture must not
# outlive the install, or a user with no serial cable is told to keep waiting
# for a board that has stopped.
splash_fail() {
	splash_pulse_stop; splash_clear_line; splash_led_set 1
	splash_hdmi_say --clear \
		'MiSTer first-time setup: FAILED' \
		'' \
		'Nothing was installed. The board itself is fine.' \
		'Re-flash sdcard.img to this card and try again.' \
		'Details are on the serial console.'
}
splash_halt_ok() {
	splash_pulse_stop; splash_clear_line; splash_led_set 1
	splash_hdmi_say --clear \
		'MiSTer first-time setup' \
		'' \
		'This card is already set up.' \
		'Power the board off and on again to start MiSTer.' \
		'(Re-flash sdcard.img only if you want to reinstall.)'
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HDMI splash -- itsalive (ADR 0020 section 9). Read THE HDMI SPLASH above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every path here ends in a log line and a return 0. The binary is probed for,
# every call runs under `timeout`, and no exit status is ever acted on beyond
# deciding whether to try the next call.
SPLASH_HDMI_BIN=/usr/bin/itsalive
SPLASH_HDMI_IMAGE_DIR=/usr/share/mister-installer   # splash-<WxH>.raw.gz, gzipped raw BGRX8888
SPLASH_HDMI_CURSOR=/sys/class/graphics/fbcon/cursor_blink
SPLASH_HDMI_OUT=/run/splash-hdmi.out  # the tool's own stderr, replayed as log lines
SPLASH_HDMI_TIMEOUT=15                # seconds per call. itsalive bounds its own
                                      # mailbox wait far tighter (exit 11); this is
                                      # the belt for the failure it did not foresee
splash_hdmi=0                         # 1 once `itsalive up` has succeeded this boot
splash_hdmi_mode=720p                 # 720p (default) or 480p; see the cmdline parse

# The tool writes its diagnostics to stderr -- "adv7513: 92 registers written",
# "no bitstream in the fabric" -- and they are exactly what a serial-console
# user wants when the screen stays dark. Replay them as installer lines so they
# land in the log in order and never tear the animated status line.
splash_hdmi_log() { splash_clear_line; printf '[installer] hdmi: %s\n' "$*"; splash_line; }
splash_hdmi_replay() {
	[ -s "$SPLASH_HDMI_OUT" ] || return 0
	while read -r _l; do splash_hdmi_log "$_l"; done < "$SPLASH_HDMI_OUT"
}

# splash_hdmi_run ARGS... -- the ONLY way this file invokes itsalive. 127 when
# the binary is absent, otherwise the tool's exit status (or timeout's).
splash_hdmi_run() {
	[ -x "$SPLASH_HDMI_BIN" ] || return 127
	timeout "$SPLASH_HDMI_TIMEOUT" "$SPLASH_HDMI_BIN" "$@"
}

# The image file for the mode: the artwork is pre-rendered per mode because the
# tool blits raw pixels and neither scales nor decodes (ADR 0020 section 9).
splash_hdmi_image() {
	case "$splash_hdmi_mode" in
	480p) printf '%s/splash-640x480.raw.gz'  "$SPLASH_HDMI_IMAGE_DIR" ;;
	*)    printf '%s/splash-1280x720.raw.gz' "$SPLASH_HDMI_IMAGE_DIR" ;;
	esac
}

# splash_hdmi_say [--clear] LINE... -- one `itsalive say` per LINE, which fbcon
# paints into the live framebuffer. A no-op until `up` has succeeded: before
# that /dev/tty1 is a console nothing is scanning out. `--clear` erases the
# picture first, so it is only passed by the terminal states.
splash_hdmi_say() {
	[ "$splash_hdmi" = 1 ] || return 0
	_clear=""
	if [ "${1:-}" = "--clear" ]; then _clear="--clear"; shift; fi
	for _line in "$@"; do
		# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # $_clear is empty or exactly one flag
		splash_hdmi_run say $_clear "$_line" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
		_clear=""
	done
	return 0
}

# Bring the screen up and put the artwork on it. Called ONCE, as the first
# thing /init does after the three early mounts -- ahead of the serial banner
# and the LED, because every moment before `up` is black screen for a user
# with no serial cable. The fabric already holds menu.rbf from U-Boot, so
# nothing later in the install makes this more likely to succeed.
# `up` goes first and ALONE on the happy path: it carries the same bitstream
# guard and the same exit codes as probe (ItsAlive_MiSTer ARCHITECTURE.md §7
# guards hdmi, fb and up), so a probe in front of it would only be a second
# exec and a second i2c bus scan between power-on and the picture. probe runs
# AFTER a failed `up` instead, because its one line per finding ("no
# bitstream", "ADV7513 not found") is the diagnosis a serial user needs when
# the screen stays dark and `up` says less. Not after a timeout (124): a
# wedged tool gets one SPLASH_HDMI_TIMEOUT of the install's time, not two.
splash_hdmi_init() {
	splash_hdmi=0
	if ! [ -x "$SPLASH_HDMI_BIN" ]; then
		splash_hdmi_log "$SPLASH_HDMI_BIN not present -- no HDMI splash this boot"
		return 0
	fi
	splash_hdmi_run up --mode "$splash_hdmi_mode" > "$SPLASH_HDMI_OUT" 2>&1
	_rc=$?
	splash_hdmi_replay
	if [ "$_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
		splash_hdmi_log "up exit $_rc -- no HDMI splash this boot (the install is unaffected)"
		if [ "$_rc" -ne 124 ]; then
			splash_hdmi_run probe > "$SPLASH_HDMI_OUT" 2>&1
			splash_hdmi_replay
		fi
		return 0
	fi
	splash_hdmi=1
	# fbcon would otherwise repaint a blinking cursor block over the artwork
	# about once a second. The knob does not survive the reboot, so it is set
	# every boot and never restored.
	printf '0' > "$SPLASH_HDMI_CURSOR" 2>/dev/null || true
	_img="$(splash_hdmi_image)"
	if [ -f "$_img" ]; then
		# zcat | image -: the raw frame (3.6 MB at 720p) never exists
		# uncompressed anywhere but the tool's stdin. `image` exiting 0 means the
		# bytes reached /dev/fb0, which after a successful `up` is the screen.
		zcat "$_img" 2> /dev/null | splash_hdmi_run image - > "$SPLASH_HDMI_OUT" 2>&1
		_rc=$?
		splash_hdmi_replay
		if [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
			splash_hdmi_log "splash on screen ($splash_hdmi_mode)"
			return 0
		fi
		splash_hdmi_log "image exit $_rc -- screen is up but blank; showing text instead"
	else
		splash_hdmi_log "no $_img -- screen is up; showing text instead"
	fi
	splash_hdmi_say --clear \
		'MiSTer first-time setup' \
		'' \
		'INSTALLING...  Reformatting your SD card.' \
		'DO NOT POWER OFF.' \
		'It reboots itself when it is done. Give it about a minute.'
	return 0
}
# >>> SPLASH SECTION END <<<

log() {
	splash_clear_line
	printf '[installer] %s\n' "$*"
	splash_line
}

# PID 1 must never exit (that is an instant kernel panic), so every terminal
# path -- the failure path AND the benign "already installed" halt -- ends by
# respawning a console shell forever. Factored out so the two callers share it.
respawn_shell() {
	while :; do
		setsid cttyhack /bin/sh || /bin/sh || sleep 5
	done
}

# Any fatal error lands here. Never panic silently. Print everything a
# serial-console user needs to tell WHERE in the install we died (the $stage
# string), what the card looks like now, and how to get back to a known-good
# state, then respawn a shell forever. The system is NOT installed.
rescue() {
	# Stop the heartbeat and park the LED solid-on BEFORE printing: a still-running
	# child would otherwise redraw its status line over this banner, and the LED
	# would keep cheerfully blinking "all is well" next to a failed install.
	splash_fail
	printf '\n'
	printf '################################################################\n'
	printf '#  MiSTer INSTALLER: FAILED\n'
	printf '#  %s\n' "$*"
	printf '#  (failed during: %s)\n' "$stage"
	printf '################################################################\n'
	printf '  cmdline    : %s\n' "$cmdline"
	printf '  root=      : %s\n' "$root_arg"
	printf '  disk       : %s\n' "$DISK"
	printf '  data part  : %s  (as %s)\n' "$DATA_PART" "$fat_type"
	printf '  new p1/p2  : %s / %s\n' "$P1" "$P2"
	printf '  cores kept : %s\n' "$([ "$cores_skipped" = yes ] && echo no || echo yes)"
	printf -- '--- /proc/partitions ---\n';  cat /proc/partitions   2>/dev/null
	printf -- '--- /proc/mounts ---\n';       cat /proc/mounts       2>/dev/null
	printf -- '--- last kernel messages ---\n'; dmesg 2>/dev/null | tail -n 25
	printf '\n'
	printf 'RECOVERY: the safest fix is to re-flash sdcard.img to this card with\n'
	printf 'Etcher or dd and boot again -- that restores a known-good installer.\n'
	printf 'If the card had already been reformatted below, re-flashing is REQUIRED\n'
	printf 'before it will boot again.\n\n'
	printf 'Dropping to a rescue shell on the console. Nothing is installed.\n\n'
	respawn_shell
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ TEST HOOK
# stop_after TAG -- simulate a power cut at a named point in the install, so that
# scripts/test-installer-recovery.sh can prove the RECOVERABILITY invariants in
# the header actually hold. It flushes what we have written so far and reboots
# immediately, leaving the card in exactly the state that point is supposed to
# leave it in.
#
# THE CMDLINE IS NOT A SAFE GATE ON ITS OWN, so it is not the gate. An earlier
# revision of this hook fired on the cmdline token alone and called that "inert on
# real hardware". It is not: `mmcboot` interpolates $v into bootargs
# (docs/boot-chain.md §4), `scrtest` `env import -t`s /linux/u-boot.txt off p1
# BEFORE mmcboot runs, and $v is the documented user knob (boot-chain §5 spells
# out that arbitrary extra tokens can therefore appear in /proc/cmdline). The
# shipped installer p1 is a plain FAT32 partition any user can write to, and
# scripts/mk-sdcard.sh documents dropping a u-boot.txt on it as supported. So a
# hand-written `v=loglevel=4 mister_installer_stop_after=recoverable` really does
# reach this script -- and stopping at `recoverable` or `payload-partial` leaves
# a card needing a re-flash, i.e. exactly the harm this ordering exists to remove.
#
# THE GATE IS A MARKER FILE IN THE INITRAMFS, /.installer-test-hook, which no
# shipped image contains: scripts/lib/installer-qemu-kernel.sh appends it as a
# second cpio archive when it builds the harness kernel, leaving the installer
# cpio and this file byte-for-byte unchanged. A user can write anything they like
# to the card and never obtain it -- changing the initramfs means rebuilding the
# kernel. The cmdline token then only chooses WHICH point to stop at.
#
# The tags, all five of them exercised by that harness (keep it that way; a tag
# nothing stops at is a branch nothing tests):
#   bootloader      after uboot.img is in the new 0xA2 partition
#   recoverable     after this installer's own kernel is back on the new p1
#   payload-partial part-way through the copy-back, so mister-payload.part/ stays
#   payload-copied  after the copy-back is renamed to mister-payload/
#   expanded        after linux/linux.img exists -- the state that used to fool
#                   the old re-run guard
stop_after() {
	[ -e /.installer-test-hook ] || return 0
	[ "$stop_after_stage" = "$1" ] || return 0
	sync
	splash_pulse_stop
	splash_clear_line
	printf '[installer] TEST-STOP after "%s" -- simulating a power cut here\n' "$1"
	sync
	reboot -f
	rescue "reboot returned after the test stop at $1"
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------- early mounts
# devtmpfs is not auto-mounted for an initramfs; everything below needs /dev.
mkdir -p /proc /sys /dev /mnt/src /mnt/dst "$RAM_DIR" /run/preseed
mount -t proc     -o nosuid,nodev,noexec proc     /proc || rescue "cannot mount /proc"
mount -t sysfs    -o nosuid,nodev,noexec sysfs    /sys  || rescue "cannot mount /sys"
mount -t devtmpfs -o nosuid              devtmpfs /dev  || rescue "cannot mount /dev"

# ------------------------------------------------------- parse /proc/cmdline
# Same discipline as stage-1: cmdline-driven, never positional, never globbing.
# Parsed BEFORE anything is drawn because the HDMI splash's mode knob lives
# here; the parse is pure shell and costs nothing, and the checks that can fail
# on its result (root=) wait until the console splash is up to say so.
stage="parsing the kernel command line"
cmdline="$(cat /proc/cmdline)"
set -f
# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # deliberate word splitting: that IS the parse
set -- $cmdline
for tok in "$@"; do
	case "$tok" in
	root=*) root_arg="${tok#root=}" ;;
	# The test hook (stop_after above). A user CAN get this token onto the
	# cmdline via u-boot.txt's $v, so parsing it is not what enables it --
	# stop_after() refuses to act without the initramfs marker file.
	mister_installer_stop_after=*) stop_after_stage="${tok#mister_installer_stop_after=}" ;;
	# The HDMI splash's one knob: 480p for a sink that will not take 720p. A
	# user reaches it through linux/u-boot.txt's $v on the shipped card
	# (docs/user/sdcard-flashing.md). Harmless by construction -- it picks
	# between two video modes and nothing else -- so, unlike the test hook
	# above, the cmdline alone is an acceptable gate for it.
	mister_installer_video=*) splash_hdmi_mode="${tok#mister_installer_video=}" ;;
	esac
done
set +f
case "$splash_hdmi_mode" in
720p|480p) ;;
*) log "ignoring mister_installer_video=\"$splash_hdmi_mode\" (720p or 480p); using 720p"
   splash_hdmi_mode=720p ;;
esac

# Picture FIRST -- before the serial banner, the LED and anything that can take
# seconds. It needs only /dev and /sys (mounted above) and the mode (parsed
# above); from here on a user with no serial cable is told what is happening
# and what not to do. Its log lines print plain (splash_tty is still 0).
splash_hdmi_init

# The console splash needs /proc (uptime), /sys (the LED) and /run (the
# heartbeat flag, created by the mkdir above), so it can only come up once
# those exist. Before this point every drawing routine is a deliberate no-op
# -- see splash_tty.
splash_init
splash_step 1 "checking the card"
[ -n "$root_arg" ] || rescue "no root= on the kernel command line"
if [ -n "$stop_after_stage" ]; then
	if [ -e /.installer-test-hook ]; then
		log "TEST HOOK ACTIVE: will stop after \"$stop_after_stage\""
	else
		log "ignoring mister_installer_stop_after=\"$stop_after_stage\": this is not a test image"
	fi
fi

# --------------------------------------------------- resolve the data partition
# root= points at the FAT data partition (p1). Support a device path, or
# LABEL=/UUID= via findfs (mirrors stage-1). Wait for slow SD/USB enumeration.
resolve_data_part() {
	case "$root_arg" in
	UUID=*|LABEL=*) findfs "$root_arg" 2>/dev/null ;;
	*)              [ -b "$root_arg" ] && printf '%s' "$root_arg" ;;
	esac
}
stage="waiting for the data partition"
waited=0
while :; do
	DATA_PART="$(resolve_data_part)"
	[ -n "$DATA_PART" ] && [ -b "$DATA_PART" ] && break
	[ "$waited" -ge "$DEV_WAIT" ] && rescue "data partition '$root_arg' never appeared"
	[ "$waited" = 0 ] && log "waiting up to ${DEV_WAIT}s for $root_arg ..."
	splash_tick   # this loop already sleeps 1, so it animates without a child
	sleep 1; waited=$((waited + 1))
done
log "data partition = $DATA_PART (after ${waited}s)"

# ------------------------------------------------- derive the WHOLE-DISK device
# NEVER repartition the wrong disk. Find the parent disk of DATA_PART via sysfs:
# the disk is the /sys/block/<disk>/ directory that CONTAINS the partition's dir.
# This is authoritative across mmcblk/sd/nvme/usb and needs no name parsing.
stage="identifying the boot disk"
partname="${DATA_PART#/dev/}"
disk_name=""
for cand in /sys/block/*/"$partname"; do
	[ -d "$cand" ] || continue
	# cand = /sys/block/<disk>/<partname>. basename/dirname are NOT built into the
	# installer busybox (installer-busybox.config), so peel the path with POSIX
	# parameter expansion instead: strip the trailing /<partname>, then keep the
	# final path element (the whole-disk name).
	cand_disk="${cand%/*}"
	disk_name="${cand_disk##*/}"
	break
done
[ -n "$disk_name" ] || rescue "could not find the parent disk of $DATA_PART in sysfs"
DISK="/dev/$disk_name"
[ -b "$DISK" ] || rescue "derived disk $DISK is not a block device"

# Partition-node naming: kernel appends 'p<N>' when the disk name ends in a digit
# (mmcblk0 -> mmcblk0p1, nvme0n1 -> nvme0n1p1), else '<N>' (sda -> sda1).
partdev() {
	case "$1" in
	*[0-9]) printf '%sp%s' "$1" "$2" ;;
	*)      printf '%s%s'  "$1" "$2" ;;
	esac
}
P1="$(partdev "$DISK" 1)"
P2="$(partdev "$DISK" 2)"
# Sanity: the data partition we were handed must actually be p1 of this disk.
# The stock U-Boot only ever boots from partition 1, so anything else means the
# card is not laid out the way this installer requires -- refuse rather than guess.
[ "$DATA_PART" = "$P1" ] || rescue "data partition $DATA_PART is not p1 ($P1) of $DISK"
log "disk=$DISK  p1(data)=$P1  p2(boot)=$P2"

# --------------------------------------------------- mount the source read-only
# READ-ONLY: we only ever read /mister-payload from here, and we must not dirty a
# partition we are about to destroy. exfat first then vfat (stock-init ordering);
# the shipped card is FAT32 so vfat wins, but exfat-first also lets the re-run
# guard below recognise an already-installed card.
stage="mounting the source data partition"
if mount -t exfat -o ro "$DATA_PART" /mnt/src 2>/dev/null; then
	fat_type="exfat"
elif mount -t vfat -o ro "$DATA_PART" /mnt/src 2>/dev/null; then
	fat_type="vfat"
else
	rescue "cannot mount $DATA_PART as exfat or vfat"
fi
log "source mounted ($fat_type)"

# ------------------------------------------------------ what is on this card?
# One decision point, because the three interesting answers are mutually exclusive
# and only one of them may reformat. The states are the ones the header's
# RECOVERABILITY section defines; read that before changing any condition here.
#
# The PRIMARY re-run guard is still structural: a finished install replaces this
# installer's linux/zImage_dtb with the real kernel, so on a good card U-Boot never
# loads us again. Everything below is belt for the cards that DID get interrupted.
stage="assessing the card"
SRC_PAYLOAD="/mnt/src/$PAYLOAD_DIR"
SRC_PAYLOAD_PART="/mnt/src/$PAYLOAD_PART_DIR"
label="$(blkid -o value -s LABEL "$DATA_PART" 2>/dev/null || true)"

# 1. ALREADY PROVISIONED. exFAT, labelled MiSTer_Data, no payload directory under
#    EITHER name, and a real linux/linux.img at the root. Re-running would DESTROY
#    a good card, so do not reformat.
#
#    The "no payload directory" clause is load-bearing and was added with the
#    recoverable ordering. Without it this guard fires MID-COMMIT: from the moment
#    linux/linux.img has been expanded until the real kernel is renamed into place,
#    an interrupted card is exFAT, labelled, and has linux.img -- while its
#    linux/zImage_dtb is still the installer's. The old guard would have called
#    that card "installed" and halted, on a card that in fact still had a complete
#    payload sitting right there and would have finished on its own.
#
#    We cannot "boot MiSTer" from this throwaway installer OS, so the safe,
#    non-destructive action is to stop and say so clearly rather than risk the
#    user's data or spin a reboot loop.
if [ "$fat_type" = "exfat" ] && [ "$label" = "$DATA_LABEL" ] &&
   [ ! -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD" ] && [ ! -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD_PART" ] && [ -f /mnt/src/linux/linux.img ]; then
	umount /mnt/src 2>/dev/null || true
	splash_halt_ok
	printf '\n################################################################\n'
	printf '#  MiSTer INSTALLER: card already provisioned -- NOT reformatting\n'
	printf '################################################################\n'
	printf '  %s is exFAT "%s" and already contains linux/linux.img,\n' "$DATA_PART" "$DATA_LABEL"
	printf '  with no installer payload left on it.\n'
	printf '  Refusing to reformat an installed card (your data is safe).\n'
	printf '  If you meant to reinstall, re-flash sdcard.img first.\n\n'
	# NOT rescue(): this is a benign, correct halt, not a failure. rescue()'s
	# "INSTALLER: FAILED / Nothing is installed" banner would be actively wrong
	# here (the card IS installed). We just can't hand off to MiSTer from this
	# throwaway installer OS, so drop to a console shell with the accurate
	# message above rather than reboot-looping into this same installer.
	printf 'Dropping to a console shell. The card is fine -- power-cycle into it,\n'
	printf 'or re-flash sdcard.img only if you actually want to reinstall.\n\n'
	respawn_shell
fi

# 2. INTERRUPTED DURING THE COPY-BACK. mister-payload.part/ is the name the
#    copy-back writes under; it is renamed to mister-payload/ only once every byte
#    is on the card. Seeing .part and no finished payload therefore means the
#    install was cut short while copying -- and the bytes it was copying FROM lived
#    in the RAM tmpfs of a machine that has since been power-cycled. There is
#    nothing left on this card to install from and nothing we can repair. Say so
#    honestly and stop; a reboot here would loop forever.
if [ -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD_PART" ] && [ ! -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD" ]; then
	umount /mnt/src 2>/dev/null || true
	splash_fail
	printf '\n################################################################\n'
	printf '#  MiSTer INSTALLER: the previous install was INTERRUPTED\n'
	printf '################################################################\n'
	printf '  %s holds %s/, a partial copy.\n' "$DATA_PART" "$PAYLOAD_PART_DIR"
	printf '  The install was cut short while writing files to the card, and\n'
	printf '  the files it was copying from were held in RAM -- they are gone.\n'
	printf '  There is nothing left on this card to install from.\n\n'
	printf 'RECOVERY: re-flash sdcard.img to this card with Etcher or dd and boot\n'
	printf 'again. Nothing else is needed, and nothing on the board was harmed.\n\n'
	printf 'Dropping to a console shell. Nothing is installed.\n\n'
	respawn_shell
fi

# --------------------------------------------- locate the payload on the source
splash_step 2 "reading the payload"
stage="locating the payload"
if [ ! -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD" ]; then
	# No payload under either name. Three ways to get here, and they want three
	# different sentences -- "wrong card?" on a card that is plainly ours, halfway
	# through our own install, is the kind of message that sends a user hunting
	# for a hardware fault. Ordered most-specific first.
	if [ -f /mnt/src/linux/linux.img ]; then
		rescue "no /$PAYLOAD_DIR on $DATA_PART but linux/linux.img is there -- an install was interrupted at its very last step; re-flash sdcard.img"
	fi
	if [ "$fat_type" = "exfat" ] && [ "$label" = "$DATA_LABEL" ]; then
		rescue "no /$PAYLOAD_DIR on an already-reformatted $DATA_PART -- an install was interrupted before the payload was copied; re-flash sdcard.img"
	fi
	rescue "payload dir /$PAYLOAD_DIR not found on $DATA_PART (wrong card?)"
fi
# The files without which the reformatted card cannot boot at all. linux.img ships
# GZIPPED (linux/linux.img.gz) -- see the MEMORY note in the header; it is expanded to
# linux/linux.img onto the exFAT card once the payload is safely on it (step 9).
for f in linux/linux.img.gz linux/zImage_dtb linux/uboot.img; do
	[ -f "$SRC_PAYLOAD/$f" ] && continue
	if [ -f /mnt/src/linux/linux.img ]; then
		rescue "/$PAYLOAD_DIR is missing $f and linux/linux.img is already there -- an install was interrupted while finishing; re-flash sdcard.img"
	fi
	rescue "payload is missing $f -- refusing to reformat"
done

# ------------------------------------ the two files that make the card recover
# NOT part of mister-payload/: the FAT root's own linux/zImage_dtb is the kernel
# running this very script, and menu.rbf is what U-Boot's fpgaload puts in the
# fabric (ADR 0020 §7). Step 6 writes both onto the freshly formatted partition
# before anything else, which is what turns "the card is mid-install" from a brick
# into a card that simply runs the installer again. Neither is fatal by absence --
# a card without them still installs correctly, it just cannot recover from an
# interruption -- so both are probed and warned about, never assumed.
stage="checking the installer's own kernel"
SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL="/mnt/src/linux/zImage_dtb"
SRC_MENU_RBF="/mnt/src/menu.rbf"
[ -f "$SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL" ] ||
	log "WARNING: no linux/zImage_dtb at the card root -- an interruption below will need a re-flash"
[ -f "$SRC_MENU_RBF" ] ||
	log "WARNING: no menu.rbf at the card root -- the fabric stays unconfigured on a re-run"

# --------------------------------------------------- stash any user pre-seed (2)
# Optional files a user dropped at the root of the FAT card before first boot,
# mirroring mr-fusion's pre-seed hooks. Copied to RAM now (before we unmount the
# source) and laid onto the finished card later. All optional; absence is normal.
stage="collecting user pre-seed"
for item in wpa_supplicant.conf _wpa_supplicant.conf samba.sh _samba.sh Scripts config; do
	if [ -e "/mnt/src/$item" ]; then
		if cp -a "/mnt/src/$item" "/run/preseed/$item" 2>/dev/null; then
			log "pre-seed: captured $item"
		else
			log "pre-seed: WARNING could not capture $item (ignored)"
		fi
	fi
done

# ------------------------------------------------ copy the payload into RAM (3)
# Peak RAM use = size of the tmpfs copy (the copy-back target is the SD card, not
# RAM). Size a tmpfs to (MemAvailable - reserve) so a mis-estimate fails with a
# clean ENOSPC instead of OOM-hanging the box. Then decide, from a real du of the
# source, whether the FULL payload fits or we must skip cores.
stage="measuring RAM"
avail_kb=0
while read -r key val _; do
	[ "$key" = "MemAvailable:" ] && { avail_kb="$val"; break; }
done < /proc/meminfo
case "$avail_kb" in ''|*[!0-9]*) avail_kb=0 ;; esac
budget_kb=$((avail_kb - RAM_RESERVE_KB))
[ "$budget_kb" -gt 0 ] || rescue "not enough free RAM to stage the payload (avail=${avail_kb}KB)"

mount -t tmpfs -o "size=${budget_kb}k" tmpfs "$RAM_DIR" ||
	rescue "cannot mount the payload tmpfs (${budget_kb}KB)"
mkdir -p "$RAM_PAYLOAD" "$RAM_INSTALLER" || rescue "cannot create the RAM staging dirs"

# du each piece separately. The two recovery files are NOT under $SRC_PAYLOAD, so
# they have to be measured and budgeted for explicitly -- they are small (~15 MB
# together) but they are also the ones we refuse to drop, so they belong in the
# base figure the cores are weighed against.
payload_kb="$(du -sk "$SRC_PAYLOAD" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)"
cores_kb=0
[ -d "$SRC_PAYLOAD/$CORES_DIR" ] && cores_kb="$(du -sk "$SRC_PAYLOAD/$CORES_DIR" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)"
recover_kb=0
[ -f "$SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL" ] && recover_kb="$(du -sk "$SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)"
rbf_kb=0
[ -f "$SRC_MENU_RBF" ] && rbf_kb="$(du -sk "$SRC_MENU_RBF" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)"
case "$payload_kb" in ''|*[!0-9]*) payload_kb=0 ;; esac
case "$cores_kb"   in ''|*[!0-9]*) cores_kb=0 ;;   esac
case "$recover_kb" in ''|*[!0-9]*) recover_kb=0 ;; esac
case "$rbf_kb"     in ''|*[!0-9]*) rbf_kb=0 ;;     esac
recover_kb=$((recover_kb + rbf_kb))
full_kb=$((payload_kb + recover_kb))
base_kb=$((full_kb - cores_kb))
log "payload: full=${full_kb}KB (cores=${cores_kb}KB, recovery=${recover_kb}KB, base=${base_kb}KB); RAM budget=${budget_kb}KB"

skip_cores=0
if [ "$full_kb" -gt "$budget_kb" ]; then
	skip_cores=1
	cores_skipped="yes"
	if [ "$base_kb" -gt "$budget_kb" ]; then
		rescue "even the base payload (${base_kb}KB) exceeds the RAM budget (${budget_kb}KB)"
	fi
	printf '\n****************************************************************\n'
	printf '*  RAM is too small to stage the _Console cores this boot.\n'
	printf '*  Installing the BASE system only; SKIPPING cores.\n'
	printf '*  Run update_all.sh after first boot to fetch cores.\n'
	printf '****************************************************************\n\n'
fi

splash_step 3 "copying to memory"
stage="copying the payload into RAM"
copy_payload_to_ram() {
	# Copy each top-level entry; skip the cores dir when RAM is tight.
	for entry in "$SRC_PAYLOAD"/* "$SRC_PAYLOAD"/.[!.]*; do
		[ -e "$entry" ] || continue
		name="${entry##*/}"
		if [ "$skip_cores" = 1 ] && [ "$name" = "$CORES_DIR" ]; then
			log "skipping cores dir $name (RAM)"
			continue
		fi
		cp -a "$entry" "$RAM_PAYLOAD"/ || return 1
	done
	# The recovery pair, into their own directory so the copy-back glob above can
	# never see them. Missing files were already warned about; not fatal.
	if [ -f "$SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL" ]; then
		cp -a "$SRC_INSTALLER_KERNEL" "$RAM_INSTALLER/zImage_dtb" || return 1
	fi
	if [ -f "$SRC_MENU_RBF" ]; then
		cp -a "$SRC_MENU_RBF" "$RAM_INSTALLER/menu.rbf" || return 1
	fi
	return 0
}
# One long, silent, blocking command: bracket it with the heartbeat child so the
# console clock keeps ticking and the LED keeps blinking while it runs.
splash_pulse_start
copy_payload_to_ram || rescue "copying the payload into RAM failed (out of space?)"
splash_pulse_stop

# Verify the RAM copy is complete for the boot-critical files BEFORE we destroy
# anything on disk. If this trips, the card is still a bootable installer. linux.img
# is present here only in its gzipped form (linux/linux.img.gz); it is expanded onto
# the card once the payload is safely there (step 9).
for f in linux/linux.img.gz linux/zImage_dtb linux/uboot.img; do
	[ -f "$RAM_PAYLOAD/$f" ] || rescue "RAM payload is missing $f after copy -- aborting before reformat"
done
log "payload staged in RAM OK"
if [ -f "$RAM_INSTALLER/zImage_dtb" ]; then
	log "installer kernel staged in RAM -- an interruption below re-runs this installer"
else
	log "WARNING: installer kernel NOT staged -- an interruption below will need a re-flash"
fi

# We are done reading the card. Everything destructive is below this line.
stage="unmounting the source"
umount /mnt/src || rescue "cannot unmount $DATA_PART before repartitioning"
sync

# ============================================================================
#  POINT OF NO RETURN -- from here the card is being rewritten.
# ============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------- repartition to full size (4)
# p1 = exFAT data from the 1 MiB-aligned start to the reserved tail; p2 = 0xA2 boot,
# EXACTLY RESERVED_SECTORS at the very end. Adapted from mr-fusion's
# S99install-MiSTer.sh, but with EXPLICIT starts+sizes instead of its
# empty-field "; size; 07" / "; ; a2" form. That form let sfdisk auto-place p1
# at LBA 2048 and gave p2 "the rest" -- which is RESERVED_SECTORS *minus* p1's
# 2048-sector start offset (6144, not 8192), so the 0xA2 boot partition came out
# 3 MiB instead of the intended 4 MiB and its size drifted with p1's alignment.
# Computing all three numbers here makes the layout deterministic and makes p2
# match genimage-sdcard.cfg's `size = 4M` on the shipped image byte-for-byte.
splash_step 4 "repartitioning the card"
stage="repartitioning $DISK"
dev_sectors="$(cat "/sys/block/$disk_name/size" 2>/dev/null)"
case "$dev_sectors" in ''|*[!0-9]*) rescue "cannot read /sys/block/$disk_name/size" ;; esac
data_sectors=$((dev_sectors - P1_START_SECTORS - RESERVED_SECTORS))
p2_start=$((dev_sectors - RESERVED_SECTORS))
[ "$data_sectors" -gt 0 ] || rescue "device too small: $dev_sectors sectors"
log "card=$dev_sectors sectors; p1=[$P1_START_SECTORS,+$data_sectors) exfat; p2=[$p2_start,+$RESERVED_SECTORS) 0xA2"

# `label: dos` forces a fresh MBR (never GPT), regardless of what the shipped
# card carried. `type=7` = exFAT/NTFS (mr-fusion parity), `type=a2` = the SPL
# boot partition. Explicit start+size on BOTH lines -> no auto-placement drift.
sfdisk --force "$DISK" <<EOF || rescue "sfdisk failed -- card partition table may be inconsistent"
label: dos
start=${P1_START_SECTORS}, size=${data_sectors}, type=7
start=${p2_start}, size=${RESERVED_SECTORS}, type=a2
EOF
sync

# Make the kernel re-read the new table and wait for the nodes to appear.
blockdev --rereadpt "$DISK" 2>/dev/null || true
stage="waiting for new partition nodes"
waited=0
while :; do
	[ -b "$P1" ] && [ -b "$P2" ] && break
	[ "$waited" -ge "$DEV_WAIT" ] && rescue "new partitions $P1 / $P2 never appeared after sfdisk"
	splash_tick   # ditto -- free animation, no heartbeat child needed here
	sleep 1; waited=$((waited + 1))
done
log "new partitions present: $P1 (data), $P2 (boot)"

# ------------------------------------- dd uboot.img to the new 0xA2 p2 (5)
# THE most brick-critical step, and the FIRST thing done after the table changes.
# sfdisk has just moved the 0xA2 partition to the tail of the medium, and until
# this dd succeeds there is no SPL where the BootROM looks (docs/boot-chain.md
# §2.1) -- the card cannot reach U-Boot at all. This used to be the LAST step of
# the install, which made the whole ~1-minute run one unbroken brick window
# (issue #185, header RECOVERABILITY). It is here now; keep it here.
#
# We dd from the RAM copy (guaranteed present -- verified above) and check the
# exit status; failure is a loud rescue, NOT a reboot.
splash_step 5 "writing the bootloader"
stage="writing uboot.img to the boot partition ($P2)"
splash_pulse_start
dd if="$RAM_PAYLOAD/linux/uboot.img" of="$P2" bs=64k 2>/dev/null ||
	rescue "dd of uboot.img to $P2 FAILED -- card is NOT bootable; re-flash sdcard.img"
sync; sync
splash_pulse_stop
log "uboot.img written to $P2 -- the card can reach U-Boot again"
stop_after bootloader

# --------------------------------------------------- mkfs.exfat on new p1 (6)
splash_step 6 "formatting (exFAT)"
stage="formatting $P1 as exFAT"
mkfs.exfat -n "$DATA_LABEL" "$P1" || rescue "mkfs.exfat on $P1 failed"
sync

stage="mounting the new data partition"
mount -t exfat -o rw,noatime "$P1" /mnt/dst || rescue "cannot mount new exFAT $P1"

# ------------------------------- put THIS installer back on the card first (7)
# The second half of the recoverability pair. U-Boot reads $core (menu.rbf) and
# then linux/zImage_dtb from p1's root; writing the installer's own copies of both
# here, before a single payload byte, means every power cut from this line until
# the commit point lands the user back in this installer rather than nowhere.
#
# Cost: ~15 MB written once. The real kernel is renamed over this one at the very
# end (the commit point), so nothing here survives a completed install.
splash_step 7 "making the card recoverable"
stage="writing the installer kernel onto $P1"
mkdir -p /mnt/dst/linux || rescue "cannot create linux/ on $P1"
if [ -f "$RAM_INSTALLER/zImage_dtb" ]; then
	cp -a "$RAM_INSTALLER/zImage_dtb" /mnt/dst/linux/zImage_dtb ||
		rescue "cannot write the installer kernel to linux/zImage_dtb on $P1"
	log "installer kernel on $P1 -- from here a power cut re-runs the installer"
else
	log "WARNING: no installer kernel to write; a power cut from here needs a re-flash"
fi
if [ -f "$RAM_INSTALLER/menu.rbf" ]; then
	if cp -a "$RAM_INSTALLER/menu.rbf" /mnt/dst/menu.rbf; then
		log "menu.rbf on $P1 (the core U-Boot fpgaload puts in the fabric)"
	else
		log "WARNING: could not write menu.rbf (ignored -- it configures the fabric, nothing more)"
	fi
fi
sync
stop_after recoverable

# ------------------------------------------- copy the payload back out of RAM (8)
# Into mister-payload.part/ first, renamed to mister-payload/ only once every
# entry is on the card. That rename is one directory-entry update on exFAT and it
# is the ONLY thing that distinguishes "this card has a payload worth installing
# from" from "this card has half a payload and no source left" on the next boot.
# Copying straight to the final name would make the two indistinguishable.
splash_step 8 "writing files to the card"
stage="writing the payload to the card"
DST_PAYLOAD="/mnt/dst/$PAYLOAD_DIR"
DST_PAYLOAD_PART="/mnt/dst/$PAYLOAD_PART_DIR"
mkdir -p "$DST_PAYLOAD_PART" || rescue "cannot create $PAYLOAD_PART_DIR on $P1"
splash_pulse_start
for entry in "$RAM_PAYLOAD"/* "$RAM_PAYLOAD"/.[!.]*; do
	[ -e "$entry" ] || continue
	cp -a "$entry" "$DST_PAYLOAD_PART/" || rescue "copying ${entry##*/} onto $P1 failed"
	stop_after payload-partial
done
sync
splash_pulse_stop

# Only rename once the files that matter are demonstrably there. A partial copy
# that kept the finished name would send the next boot into a reformat with an
# incomplete source -- the one outcome this whole ordering exists to prevent.
for f in linux/linux.img.gz linux/zImage_dtb linux/uboot.img; do
	[ -f "$DST_PAYLOAD_PART/$f" ] || rescue "$f missing on $P1 after copy-back"
done
stage="committing the payload directory"
mv "$DST_PAYLOAD_PART" "$DST_PAYLOAD" ||
	rescue "cannot rename $PAYLOAD_PART_DIR to $PAYLOAD_DIR on $P1"
sync
log "payload written to $P1 as $PAYLOAD_DIR/ -- this card can now finish on its own"
stop_after payload-copied

# ------------------------------------ expand linux.img.gz onto the card (9)
# linux.img ships GZIPPED so the 512 MiB image never has to fit the mem=511M RAM
# tmpfs (header MEMORY note). The small .gz is now on the exFAT card, inside the
# payload dir; stream-decompress it to its FINAL linux/linux.img -- zcat reads the
# .gz and writes the expansion straight to DISK, so the full 512 MiB is never held
# in RAM.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT done here: the .gz is not moved or deleted yet. It
# is the longest step in the install by a wide margin, so the payload directory is
# left complete and untouched for its whole duration -- a power cut anywhere in
# here re-runs the install unattended on the next boot. The cleanup belongs with
# the other renames, in the commit phase below.
splash_step 9 "expanding the system image"
stage="expanding linux.img onto the card"
[ -f "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/linux.img.gz" ] || rescue "linux/linux.img.gz missing on $P1 after copy-back"
# The longest single command in the whole install: 512 MiB decompressed straight
# onto SD, with no progress of its own. This is THE step users would otherwise
# sit through wondering whether the board had died.
splash_pulse_start
zcat "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/linux.img.gz" > /mnt/dst/linux/linux.img ||
	rescue "decompressing linux/linux.img.gz onto $P1 failed (card out of space, or corrupt payload)"
splash_pulse_stop
sync
stop_after expanded

# ===========================================================================
#  COMMIT PHASE (10) -- everything below is renames, and the LAST of them is
#  what turns this card from an installer into a MiSTer.
# ===========================================================================
# The window opens at the `rm` of linux/linux.img.gz immediately below -- NOT at
# the first rename. That `rm` is what first makes mister-payload/ incomplete, and
# incomplete is what the next boot's completeness probe keys on, so from there
# until the final `mv` the card is neither re-runnable nor installed: it still
# boots this installer, which halts honestly without touching anything. That is
# one of the two windows named in the header, and it is why every operation in
# this phase is a directory-entry update rather than a copy -- the whole phase is
# fractions of a second, against the ~1 minute the old ordering was exposed for.
# Do not put anything slow in here, and do not add a write above that `rm`
# expecting it to be outside the window; it would not be.
splash_step 10 "finishing the install"

# Delete the now-redundant .gz so the finished card carries only linux/linux.img
# (what U-Boot loop-mounts). Until PR #76 this `rm -f` was a silent
# command-not-found no-op -- installer-busybox.config had `# CONFIG_RM is not
# set` -- so every installed card kept the whole ~80 MiB gzip forever, and once
# there was a splash it also printed "rm: not found" across it. CONFIG_RM is now
# on (see that file's header for the costing: ~1 KB in a 13 MB RAM-resident
# initramfs).
#
# Then VERIFY, and fall back to truncating. That is the real lesson of this bug:
# the failure mode was not "rm was missing", it was "rm was missing and nothing
# noticed for a whole release". An unchecked cleanup command that silently
# no-ops is exactly what put 80 MiB on every card. If the applet ever goes away
# again this now says so on the console AND still releases the blocks.
stage="removing the compressed system image"
rm -f "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/linux.img.gz" 2>/dev/null
if [ -e "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/linux.img.gz" ]; then
	: > "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/linux.img.gz"
	log "WARNING: could not delete linux/linux.img.gz (no rm applet?) -- truncated it instead"
else
	log "removed linux/linux.img.gz"
fi

# ------------------------------- move the payload into its final places (10)
# `mv`, never `cp`: these bytes are already on this filesystem and rewriting
# 150+ MiB of them would hand back every second the reorder just bought.
#
# Two collisions are expected, both because step 7 already wrote there:
#   linux/    -- an existing NON-EMPTY directory. rename(2) refuses that
#                (ENOTEMPTY), and BusyBox mv does not fall back for it, so this
#                one directory is descended and its children moved individually.
#   menu.rbf  -- an existing regular FILE. rename(2) replaces it silently, and
#                it is byte-identical anyway (ADR 0020 §7: the FAT root copy is a
#                copy of the payload's). `mv -f` so BusyBox never prompts.
# linux/zImage_dtb is skipped here on purpose: it IS the commit point and moves
# last, on its own, below.
stage="moving the payload into place"
commit_payload() {
	for entry in "$DST_PAYLOAD"/* "$DST_PAYLOAD"/.[!.]*; do
		[ -e "$entry" ] || continue
		name="${entry##*/}"
		if [ "$name" = "linux" ]; then
			for sub in "$entry"/* "$entry"/.[!.]*; do
				[ -e "$sub" ] || continue
				[ "${sub##*/}" = "zImage_dtb" ] && continue
				mv -f "$sub" /mnt/dst/linux/ || return 1
			done
			continue
		fi
		mv -f "$entry" /mnt/dst/ || return 1
	done
	return 0
}
commit_payload || rescue "moving the payload into place on $P1 failed"
sync

# Verify what has landed so far (linux.img is the expanded one above; the real
# zImage_dtb is still in the payload dir and is checked at the commit point).
for f in linux/linux.img linux/uboot.img; do
	[ -f "/mnt/dst/$f" ] || rescue "$f missing on $P1 after the commit moves"
done
log "payload moved into place on $P1"

# ------------------------------------------------- apply user pre-seed (10)
stage="applying user pre-seed"
apply_preseed() {
	# $1 = name under /run/preseed ; $2 = destination path under /mnt/dst
	[ -e "/run/preseed/$1" ] || return 0
	# dirname is NOT built into the installer busybox: derive the parent dir with
	# POSIX parameter expansion (strip the trailing /<basename>).
	dest="/mnt/dst/$2"
	mkdir -p "${dest%/*}"
	if cp -a "/run/preseed/$1" "$dest" 2>/dev/null; then
		log "pre-seed applied: $1 -> $2"
	else
		log "pre-seed: WARNING failed to apply $1 (ignored)"
	fi
}
apply_preseed wpa_supplicant.conf  linux/wpa_supplicant.conf
apply_preseed _wpa_supplicant.conf linux/_wpa_supplicant.conf
apply_preseed samba.sh             linux/_samba.sh
apply_preseed _samba.sh            linux/_samba.sh
# Directory pre-seeds are merged (cp -a of the dir onto the existing tree).
if [ -d /run/preseed/Scripts ]; then
	if cp -a /run/preseed/Scripts /mnt/dst/; then log "pre-seed applied: Scripts/"; else log "pre-seed: WARNING Scripts/ failed (ignored)"; fi
fi
if [ -d /run/preseed/config ]; then
	if cp -a /run/preseed/config /mnt/dst/; then log "pre-seed applied: config/"; else log "pre-seed: WARNING config/ failed (ignored)"; fi
fi

# -------------------------------------- per-board MAC into u-boot.txt (10)
# Every board otherwise shares the compiled-in fallback ethaddr 02:03:04:05:06:07
# (docs/boot-chain.md §3.1 entry 14). Generate a random LOCALLY-ADMINISTERED,
# UNICAST MAC: first octet |= 0x02 (local-admin bit set) and &= 0xFE (multicast bit
# cleared). This is the direct arithmetic equivalent of mr-fusion's b="2,6,a,e"
# low-nibble trick, but readable and awk-free. u-boot.txt MUST end in a newline (see
# header) -- printf provides it.
stage="writing per-board MAC"
gen_mac() {
	# 6 random bytes as space-separated hex from hexdump; force the first octet's
	# bits. hexdump is the applet the installer busybox actually ships for exactly
	# this (installer-busybox.config header: "Picked over CONFIG_AWK ... hexdump -e
	# is the standard idiom for N random bytes as hex octets"); od is NOT built.
	# Word-splitting hexdump's output into $1..$6 is the whole point; it emits only
	# hex digits and spaces, so there is nothing to glob or expand.
	# shellcheck disable=SC2046
	set -- $(hexdump -n6 -e '6/1 "%02x "' /dev/urandom)
	[ $# -eq 6 ] || return 1
	o1=$(( (0x$1 | 0x02) & 0xFE ))
	printf '%02X:%02X:%02X:%02X:%02X:%02X' \
		"$o1" "$((0x$2))" "$((0x$3))" "$((0x$4))" "$((0x$5))" "$((0x$6))"
}
mkdir -p /mnt/dst/linux
if mac="$(gen_mac)" && [ -n "$mac" ]; then
	printf 'ethaddr=%s\n' "$mac" > /mnt/dst/linux/u-boot.txt ||
		rescue "cannot write /linux/u-boot.txt on $P1"
	log "per-board MAC = $mac"
else
	# A missing MAC is not fatal (the board still boots with the shared fallback),
	# but it IS worth shouting about so it can be fixed by hand.
	log "WARNING: could not generate a MAC; board will use the shared fallback"
fi

# ====================== THE COMMIT POINT ===================================
# One rename. Before it, linux/zImage_dtb is this installer and the card runs the
# installer again on any boot. After it, linux/zImage_dtb is the real MiSTer
# kernel and the installer is gone from the card for good. Everything else is
# already in place and flushed; nothing slow, nothing conditional, nothing that
# can half-succeed goes between the sync above and the sync below.
stage="installing the real kernel"
sync
[ -f "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/zImage_dtb" ] ||
	rescue "the payload's linux/zImage_dtb is gone before the commit -- card is NOT installed"
mv -f "$DST_PAYLOAD/linux/zImage_dtb" /mnt/dst/linux/zImage_dtb ||
	rescue "cannot install the real kernel over linux/zImage_dtb on $P1"
sync
log "real kernel installed -- this card is a MiSTer now, not an installer"

# Housekeeping, strictly AFTER the commit: the payload dir should now be empty.
# Deliberately not before -- between "payload dir gone" and "real kernel in place"
# the card would look provisioned to the guard above while still booting the
# installer, and that is a worse state than the stray directory this risks. If the
# remove fails the card is already a working MiSTer and never runs this again, so
# a leftover directory is clutter, not a defect. Say so and carry on.
stage="removing the staged payload"
rm -rf "$DST_PAYLOAD" 2>/dev/null
if [ -e "$DST_PAYLOAD" ]; then
	log "WARNING: could not remove $PAYLOAD_DIR/ from $P1 (harmless -- the card is installed)"
else
	log "removed $PAYLOAD_DIR/ from $P1"
fi

# --------------------------------------------------- flush + unmount the data fs
stage="flushing the data partition"
sync
umount /mnt/dst || rescue "cannot unmount $P1 (data not fully flushed)"
sync

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ reboot (10)
stage="rebooting into the installed MiSTer"
# Draws the completion banner and puts the LED out: from here the next thing that
# lights it is the installed system's own mmc0 trigger, one boot from now.
splash_done
log "install complete -- rebooting into MiSTer"
sync
# `reboot -f`: direct reboot(2) syscall. Plain `reboot` would try to signal an init
# process, but we ARE pid 1 and there is no init here -- it would just hang.
reboot -f

# If reboot ever returns, do not fall off the end of pid 1 (kernel panic).
rescue "reboot returned unexpectedly"
